  Every positive integer has a 2-adic M-word (no 2-adic constraint); the Z-number condition is the
  real tail condition f_n < 1/2 for all n.  For T-swap orbits it is the opposite: the real tails lie in
  I_A automatically and the whole difficulty is 2-adic integrality (B3).

==============================================================================
B7  independent check of the swap identity behind Proposition SP (Bernstein formula, mod 2^N)
==============================================================================
  Phi_T(Y_S) == -(1/3^a)[R_B/(1-rho) + (R_B'-R_B) Theta_S(rho)]  (mod 2^3000) on 15 random (L,a,B,B',S): PASS

==============================================================================
B8  Theorem M': exact equivalence for adjacent pairs (R_B' = R_B + 1) with alpha = 3^a/2^L > 2
==============================================================================
  If R_B' = R_B + 1 and p > 2q, then for I = [R_B, R_B+1]/(p-q) the integer e_j = q x_{j+1} - p x_j = p t_j - q t_{j+1}
  lies in [R_B - q/(p-q), R_B + 1 + q/(p-q)], whose only integers are R_B, R_B + 1: the real confinement
  xi alpha^j in Z + I FORCES the digits, hence the Collatz blocks.  So Z(alpha, I) <=> T1 on {B,B'}^N.
  L=10 a= 7 alpha=2187/1024=2.1357: 4 adjacent pair(s), e.g. 0111101110 (R=4726), 1101100111 (R=4727); I = [4726, 4727]/1163; |I|*p = 1.8805 (FLP needs < 1)
  L=16 a=11 alpha=177147/65536=2.7030: 5 adjacent pair(s), e.g. 0111011111100110 (R=481798), 1101111000111011 (R=481799); I = [481798, 481799]/111611; |I|*p = 1.5872 (FLP needs < 1)
  L=19 a=13 alpha=1594323/524288=3.0409: 1 adjacent pair(s), e.g. 1011111001101010111 (R=5018677), 0111011110110101110 (R=5018678); I = [5018677, 5018678]/1070035; |I|*p = 1.4900 (FLP needs < 1)
  L=20 a=14 alpha=4782969/1048576=4.5614: 8 adjacent pair(s), e.g. 11101010111111110010 (R=10674673), 01111111011110110001 (R=10674674); I = [10674673, 10674674]/3734393; |I|*p = 1.2808 (FLP needs < 1)
  (10,7), x0 <= 2^22: d=1: #S_1 (integer test) = 8192, #C_1 (T-iteration) = 8192, equal: True
  (10,7), x0 <= 2^22: d=2: S_d = [555195, 694238, 873659, 1012702, 1603771, 1742814, 1922235, 2061278, 2652347, 2791390, 2970811, 3109854, 3700923, 3839966, 4019387, 4158430]  C_d = [555195, 694238, 873659, 1012702, 1603771, 1742814, 1922235, 2061278, 2652347, 2791390, 2970811, 3109854, 3700923, 3839966, 4019387, 4158430]  equal: True
  (10,7), x0 <= 2^22: d=3: S_d = []  C_d = []  equal: True
  So, for these explicit pairs, 'no divergent Collatz orbit with parity vector eventually in {B,B'}^N' is
  EQUIVALENT to the Mahler-type statement 'no xi > 0 has xi (3^a/2^L)^j in Z + [R_B, R_B+1]/(3^a-2^L) for all j'.

