==============================================================================
P1  bijection and exact identities
==============================================================================
  U(g(n)) = g(U(n)) = n and g(-n) = -g(n) for |n| <= 1000000: PASS (g is an odd permutation of Z)
  g(n) = round(4n/3) (3 !| n);  U(m) = 3m/2 = W(m) (m even), round(3m/4) = floor(W(m)/2) (m odd),
  with W(m) = ceil(3m/2) Mahler's map: PASS on |n| <= 3000.
  odd k: U(k) = (3k+s)/4 with the unique sign s making 4 | 3k+s (Althoefer's one-player descending
  3n+-1 move); U(k) = T^2(k) = T_-((k+1)/2) for k = 1 mod 4 and U(k) = T_-^2(k) = T((k-1)/2) for k = 3 mod 4: PASS.
  g(T(n)) = 2n+1, equivalently T(n) = U(2n+1), for every odd |n| < 10^6: PASS
  g(m) = 2 T_+^{-1}(m) + 1 (m = 2 mod 3), g(m) = 2 T_-^{-1}(m) - 1 (m = 1 mod 3), g(3m) = 2m: PASS
  -> Collatz's permutation = (odd-branch predecessor on the sheet chosen by m mod 3) followed by x -> 2x+-1.
  g(3m+1) = 2 g(3m) + 1, g(3m+2) = 2 g(3m) + 3: PASS  (the triple 3m,3m+1,3m+2 -> x, 2x+1, 2x+3, x=2m)
  T^3(4m+1) = T(m) for odd m (the '4x+1 fractal recursion' of the T-tree): PASS; hence for odd m,
  T^3(g(3m+1)) = T(g(3m)/2).

==============================================================================
P2  p-adic extensions and itinerary statistics (exact)
==============================================================================
  g: for each r mod 3, {n = r mod 3} mod 3^(k+1) -> Z/3^k is a bijection (k <= 8): g extends to a
     full-branch map of Z_3 with three branches of expansion 3; Haar measure is invariant.
  U: the branches 2Z_2, 1+4Z_2, 3+4Z_2 map onto Z_2 with expansions 2, 4, 4 (k <= 11): Haar-invariant,
     itinerary letters Bernoulli(1/2, 1/4, 1/4).
  the first k letters of the ternary g-itinerary are a bijection of Z/3^k (k <= 8): Terras-type theorem.
  Haar probability that the g-orbit's log-walk ever drops below its start: 0.763108
  (the analogue of Terras's theorem FAILS: a positive-density set of integers never descends in the
   model -- the drift is positive, as for 5x+1.)

==============================================================================
P3  cycle census: all orbits meeting [1, 1000000], escape bound B = 10^24
==============================================================================
  cycle of length 1: min 1, max 1: [1]
  cycle of length 2: min 2, max 3: [2, 3]
  cycle of length 5: min 4, max 9: [4, 5, 6, 7, 9]
  cycle of length 12: min 44, max 111: [44, 59, 62, 66, 70, 74, 79, 83, 93, 99, 105, 111]
  distinct non-periodic-looking orbits meeting [1,1000000] (exceed B both ways): 49957; max steps 2688
  FINITE-EXACT: every positive g-cycle with minimum <= 1000000 and maximum <= B is one of the 4 above.

==============================================================================
P4  the orbit of 8: 100000 steps forward (g) and backward (U)
==============================================================================
  forward g: min value seen 8; ln|x_s|/s at s=1e3,1e4,1e5: 0.058224, 0.049075, 0.055909;  letter counts {0: 33441, 1: 33173, 2: 33386}
  backward U: min value seen 8; ln|x_s|/s at s=1e3,1e4,1e5: 0.052679, 0.058918, 0.061115;  letter counts {0: 50317, 1: 24860, 3: 24823}
  predicted drifts: g +0.056633 (letters mod 3 uniform), U +0.058892 (even : 1 mod 4 : 3 mod 4 = 2:1:1).
  8 is not periodic within 10^5 steps in either direction (known; the orbit of 8 is conjectured infinite).

==============================================================================
P5  typing of 'the orbit of 8 is infinite' against the foundry controls
==============================================================================
  SHEET   : g is odd (g(-n) = -g(n)): no sign asymmetry at all; but each step mixes the 3x+1 and 3x-1 odd
            branches (P1), so every sheet-specific Collatz tool is inapplicable
  DRIFT   : both directions expand (P2: +0.0566, +0.0589 per step); infinite orbits are the EXPECTED behaviour,
            as for 5x+1: the statement is an existence-of-divergence problem (reverse transfer), not a no-divergence one
  DEFECT  : a single orbit; density/Haar statements (P2) say nothing about 8
  INTEGRAL: rational cycles of g exist for every periodic ternary itinerary (full-branch map)
            so an argument must isolate the integer 8
  UNIFORM : g is an RCWA permutation; for the class of RCWA permutations cycle questions are undecidable-type
            (Kurtz-Simon type; see the note)
  LOGIC   : 'orbit of 8 infinite' <=> 'g^k(8) != 8 for all k >= 1' is Pi^0_1 (refutable by finding a cycle)
            like Goldbach/RH; 'finite' is Sigma^0_1

==============================================================================
P6  the gate shared with 3x+1: g-cycles sit on the clocks K/k of log_2 3 (exact)
==============================================================================
  A g-step is x -> (c x + d)/3 with (c,d) = (2,0), (4,-1), (4,1) for x = 0,1,2 mod 3.  Over a word w
  of length k with a zeros and b = k-a nonzero letters, g^k(x) = (2^K x + D_w)/3^k, K = a + 2b,
  D_() = 0, D_{w r} = c_r D_w + d_r 3^{|w|}.  A cycle point satisfies  n (3^k - 2^K) = D_w,
  the same gate 2^K - 3^L as a 3x+1 cycle (Boehm-Sontacchi: n (2^K - 3^L) = B(w) > 0), but with a
  SIGNED carry D_w.  Product identity: 3^k/2^K = prod over nonzero letters of (1 + d_r/(4 n_i)).
  cycle min   1: k= 1 a=0 b=1 K= 2  clock K/k=2/1  gate 3^k-2^K=    -1  D_w=      -1  n0 = D/gate: True   reduced clock 2/1 convergent: True
  cycle min   2: k= 2 a=1 b=1 K= 3  clock K/k=3/2  gate 3^k-2^K=     1  D_w=       2  n0 = D/gate: True   reduced clock 3/2 convergent: True
  cycle min   4: k= 5 a=2 b=3 K= 8  clock K/k=8/5  gate 3^k-2^K=   -13  D_w=     -52  n0 = D/gate: True   reduced clock 8/5 convergent: True
  cycle min  44: k=12 a=5 b=7 K=19  clock K/k=19/12  gate 3^k-2^K=  7153  D_w=  314732  n0 = D/gate: True   reduced clock 19/12 convergent: True
  (Shanks 1965 [R] already noted that the cycle lengths 1, 2, 5, 12 are convergent denominators of log_2 3;
   Atkin 1966 [R]: no further cycle of period < 200; Simons 2022 [P, arXiv 2205.10582]: finitely many m-cycles
   by Baker/Rhin, none for m <= 2 besides the known ones.  New here: the signed-carry comparison below.)
  unit-gap clocks (1/1, 2/1, 3/2; |3^k - 2^K| = 1 only there by Catalan/Gersonides): every word gives an
  integer cycle, and the cycles are exactly [(-3, -2), (-1,), (0,), (1,), (2, 3)]: the unit gaps carry only the known small cycles.
  3x+-1 comparison (inherited gate census): the signed 3x+-1 cycles sit on 1/1, 2/1, 3/2, 11/7
  (gates -1, 1, -1, -139); Collatz's permutation uses 2/1, 3/2, 8/5, 19/12 (gates -1, 1, -13, 7153).
  The unit (Catalan) gaps 2/1 and 3/2 carry cycles of BOTH; 13 and 7153, which no 3x+-1 carry cancels
  (all 35 words on 8/5 have q=13; min q=23 on 19/12), are cancelled by g's signed carries.
  Eliahou-type transfer: a g-cycle with every element > N0 = 1000000 has |K/k - log_2 3| < 1/((4N0-1) ln 2)
  = 3.607e-07, so k >= 665 (smallest denominator in that window, fraction 1054/665).
  With P3: any g-cycle besides the four has min > 1000000 (hence length >= 665) or max > 10^24.

DONE collatz_permutation

